BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Jacobs School of Music
DTSTART:20261210T010000Z
DTEND:20261210T030000Z
LOCATION:Auer Hall
SUMMARY:Jeremy Denk\, alumnus guest piano
DESCRIPTION:Repertoire  J. S. Bach: Prelude and Fugue in B Major\, BWV 89
 2 Unsuk Chin: Six Etudes (2003)Beethoven: Sonata in A-Flat Major\, Op. 11
 0 (1821)Montgeroult: Selections from "114 études de difficultés progres
 sives"Beethoven: Sonata in C Minor\, Op. 111 (1822)About the ArtistJeremy
  Denk is one of America's foremost pianists\, hailed by the New York Time
 s (NYT) as "a pianist you want to hear no matter what he performs" and ce
 lebrated for performances of vast imagination\, beauty\, profundity\, and
  wit. An NYT bestselling author\, Denk is the recipient of both the MacAr
 thur "Genius" Fellowship and the Avery Fisher Prize and is a member of th
 e American Academy of Arts and Sciences. This season\, Denk marks the bic
 entennial of Beethoven's death with a series of recitals across the Unite
 d States\, including appearances at Boston's Vivo Performing Arts\, Phila
 delphia Chamber Music Society\, and New York's 92nd Street Y. He also per
 forms Beethoven at Carnegie Hall with the New York String Orchestra and M
 ichael Stern\, Brahms with the Vancouver Symphony\, and Bartók with the 
 St. Louis Symphony and Dinis Sousa. Denk continues his relationship with 
 Scotland's Lammermuir Festival\, where he performs six Mozart Piano Conce
 rtos\, Schubert\, and Janáček. He also continues his collaboration with
  the Takács Quartet\, with performances including at Carnegie Hall\, Cal
  Performances\, and Santa Monica's BroadStage. Denk is celebrated for his
  original and insightful writings on music\, which New Yorker critic Alex
  Ross praised for its "arresting sensitivity and wit." His NYT bestsellin
 g memoir\, Every Good Boy Does Fine\, was published to universal acclaim 
 by Random House in 2022\, with features on CBS Sunday Morning\, NPR's Fre
 sh Air\, NYT\, and TheGuardian. He also wrote the libretto for a comic op
 era presented by Carnegie Hall\, Cal Performances\, and the Aspen Festiva
 l\, and his writing has appeared in TheNew Yorker\, New Republic\, Guardi
 an\, Süddeutsche Zeitung\, and on the front page of the NYT Book Review.
  Denk is known for his interpretations of the music of Charles Ives\, and
  in celebration of the 150th anniversary of his birth\, Nonesuch Records 
 released a collection of his Ives recordings in 2024. His recording of th
 e Beethoven sonata was selected by BBC Radio 3's Building a Library as th
 e best available version recorded on modern piano.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Repertoire</strong><br />  J. S. 
 Bach: <em>Prelude and Fugue in B Major\, BWV 892<br /></em> Unsuk Chin: <
 em>Six Etudes (2003)<br /></em>Beethoven: <em>Sonata in A-Flat Major\, Op
 . 110 (1821)<br /></em>Montgeroult: <em>Selections from "</em><em>114 ét
 udes de difficultés progressives"<br /></em>Beethoven: <em>Sonata in C M
 inor\, Op. 111 (1822)<br /></em><br /><strong>About the Artist</strong><b
 r /><br /><strong>Jeremy Denk</strong> is one of America's foremost piani
 sts\, hailed by the <em>New York Times</em> (NYT) as "a pianist you want 
 to hear no matter what he performs" and celebrated for performances of va
 st imagination\, beauty\, profundity\, and wit. An <em>NYT</em> bestselli
 ng author\, Denk is the recipient of both the MacArthur "Genius" Fellowsh
 ip and the Avery Fisher Prize and is a member of the American Academy of 
 Arts and Sciences. This season\, Denk marks the bicentennial of Beethoven
 's death with a series of recitals across the United States\, including a
 ppearances at Boston's Vivo Performing Arts\, Philadelphia Chamber Music 
 Society\, and New York's 92<sup>nd</sup> Street Y. He also performs Beeth
 oven at Carnegie Hall with the New York String Orchestra and Michael Ster
 n\, Brahms with the Vancouver Symphony\, and Bartók with the St. Louis S
 ymphony and Dinis Sousa. Denk continues his relationship with Scotland's 
 Lammermuir Festival\, where he performs six Mozart Piano Concertos\, Schu
 bert\, and Janáček. He also continues his collaboration with the Takác
 s Quartet\, with performances including at Carnegie Hall\, Cal Performanc
 es\, and Santa Monica's BroadStage. Denk is celebrated for his original a
 nd insightful writings on music\, which <em>New Yorker</em> critic Alex R
 oss praised for its "arresting sensitivity and wit." His <em>NYT</em> bes
 tselling memoir\, <em>Every Good Boy Does Fine</em>\, was published to un
 iversal acclaim by Random House in 2022\, with features on <em>CBS Sunday
  Morning</em>\, NPR's <em>Fresh Air</em>\, <em>NYT</em>\, and <em>The</em
 ><em>Guardian</em>. He also wrote the libretto for a comic opera presente
 d by Carnegie Hall\, Cal Performances\, and the Aspen Festival\, and his 
 writing has appeared in <em>The</em><em>New Yorker</em>\, <em>New Republi
 c</em>\, <em>Guardian</em>\, <em>Süddeutsche Zeitung</em>\, and on the f
 ront page of the <em>NYT Book Review</em>. Denk is known for his interpre
 tations of the music of Charles Ives\, and in celebration of the 150<sup>
 th</sup> anniversary of his birth\, Nonesuch Records released a collectio
 n of his Ives recordings in 2024. His recording of the Beethoven sonata w
 as selected by BBC Radio 3's <em>Building a Library</em> as the best avai
 lable version recorded on modern piano.</p>
